## Asscher Halo vs Asscher Solitaire: The Price Comparison

How much more does a halo cost versus a solitaire?

The halo setting itself costs $1,312–$4,489. A comparable solitaire setting costs $860–$1,240. The difference is $450–$3,250 depending on which you compare.

But the stone requirement changes too. A halo requires VS1 clarity minimum, versus VS2 for a solitaire. That clarity upgrade costs $100–$400 at the 1ct range.

Total additional cost of going halo versus solitaire: roughly $550–$700 for the mid-range comparison — Falling Edge halo at $1,807 versus Ten Prong solitaire at $860, plus clarity upgrade.

Is that worth it? It depends what you're buying the halo for.

If the goal is apparent size: yes. The halo adds 0.3–0.4ct apparent weight at a cost of $550–$700 in setting premium. That's a cheaper way to add perceived size than buying a larger stone.

If the goal is authenticity to the Art Deco tradition: the solitaire is more historically correct. Original asscher rings from the 1920s were set in simple solitaire mountings.

If the goal is maximum finger coverage and sparkle: the double halo at $4,489 is the choice — but the total ring price at that level exceeds $7,000 with a quality stone.

See the full comparison of all asscher setting styles in the [asscher cut diamond engagement rings guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings).

---

## Metal Guide for Asscher Halo Rings

Metal choice interacts with halo design in specific ways.

### White Gold (14K)

White gold is the most versatile metal for asscher halo rings. The cool tone complements the step facets' depth and makes G–H color stones appear colorless in context.

The Falling Edge Pavé Halo in 14K white gold at $1,807 is the most popular pick from this list. White gold requires replating every 2–3 years ($50–$80 at any jeweler).

### Yellow Gold (14K)

Yellow gold pairs well with H–I color asscher stones. The warm tone absorbs any slight tint in the stone, making lower color grades appear whiter in a yellow setting.

The Falling Edge Pavé Halo in yellow gold is $1,852. The Zac Zac Posen Square Halo is also available in yellow gold at $2,370.

For a full Art Deco vintage look, yellow gold with a square halo is the historically accurate combination.

### Rose Gold (14K)

Rose gold creates contrast with the asscher's geometric step facets — a deliberate tension between soft metal and hard geometry. The Classic Halo in rose gold at $1,725 is the entry option.

Rose gold works best with H–I color stones, same as yellow gold. The Double Halo Gala in rose gold at $4,489 is the premium pick.

### Platinum

Platinum is the original asscher metal. Permanently white, denser than gold, no replating required.

The Falling Edge Pavé Halo in platinum is $2,257. The Blue Nile Studio Heiress Halo in platinum is $2,689.

Platinum is right for buyers who want maximum durability and the Art Deco purity of the original material.

| Metal | Price Range | Color Grade Sweet Spot | Maintenance |
|---|---|---|---|
| 14K White Gold | $1,312–$2,257 | G–H | Replating every 2–3 years |
| 14K Yellow Gold | $1,852–$2,370 | H–I | None |
| 14K Rose Gold | $1,725–$4,489 | H–I | None |
| Platinum | $2,257–$2,689 | D–G | None |

**What this means for you:** Go white gold unless you have a strong reason not to. It's the most versatile option, costs less than platinum, and pairs with the widest range of stone grades.

