## Natural vs Lab for an Asscher Halo Setting

Halo settings favor lab diamonds more than almost any other ring style.

Here's why. A halo setting already adds sparkle and visual presence around the center stone. That additional brilliance makes even a lab stone with good clarity appear exceptional.

And lab stones give you more size, which the halo then amplifies.

In a halo setting, a larger center stone reads dramatically larger than a small center stone in a solitaire. The halo multiplies the benefit of every extra carat.

At the 1ct range, the numbers are stark:

- Natural GIA 1.00ct E-VS1: $2,464 + $1,807 setting = **$4,271** total ring
- Lab IGI 1.52ct D-IF: $2,795 + $1,807 setting = **$4,602** total ring

For $331 more, the lab ring gives you 52% more carat weight and Flawless clarity vs VS1. In a halo setting, that size difference is visible every time someone looks at the ring.

> **Farzana's take:** Halo plus lab is the highest-value combination on the market right now. I had a client last month who came in with a $5,000 budget expecting a modest ring. She left with a 1.84ct D-IF asscher in a Falling Edge Pavé Halo. In a jewelry store, that stone alone would cost $12,000–$15,000. The halo adds another layer of presence. She gets asked about her ring constantly — and she paid $5,350 all-in. That's the combination working exactly as it should.

The case for natural: if origin matters, if GIA certification from a lab with a 70-year track record is important, if long-term resale value is a factor — natural is the right call. Natural asscher halo rings from [$3,776 complete](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) are not overpriced. They're a different decision.

